PARS = [
("ILDFIRE response is carried out", """ILDFIRE response is carried out by a system of systems {1}. A hazard model turns the observations retrieved from heterogeneous sources into a forecast of where the fire will spread. Regional teams decide where to commit crews, aircraft, and evacuation orders. An operator supervises the whole under a clock set by the fire {2;3}. Fire seasons are becoming longer and more severe {2}. Moreover, the decisions that matter most are taken in the first hours. In these hours observations are sparse, the forecast is uncertain, and a wrong commitment is costly {3;4}. A recommendation after the fire has moved is useless, so the support has to adapt to the incident as it moves."""),
("DSSs exist for parts of this task", """DSSs exist for parts of this task {6}. Spread simulators forecast fire behavior from fuel, terrain, and weather {7;8}. Allocation tools assign crews and aircraft against a forecast {9;10}. Integrated platforms bring these parts together for use in the field {4;11}. However, field studies of these tools measure how widely they are used, not whether the decisions taken with them are better {13;14}. A critical review of DSS research reports the same pattern across the field. Many systems are built, and few are shown to help {15}. VODA is therefore measured as the change in the outcome of the same incidents, with and without the system."""),
("Three structural limits exist across these systems", """Three structural limits exist across these systems. First, the alternatives are predefined. A rule base, a menu of tactics, or an optimization model fixes the orders before the incident, so the system can only rank the alternatives it is given. It cannot produce an order that was not written before the incident {11}. Decision analysis has long identified the generation of alternatives as the weakest step of the process {18;20;37}. The methods proposed for it either enumerate variations of a fixed model {21} or depend on the recall of the operator under time pressure {23}. Second, uncertainty is representable only through prior distributions or belief intervals. Real-time sensing cannot supply them {24;25}, and the reasoning behind a recommendation is exposed through post-hoc attribution {26}. Third, integrated platforms embed the forecast and the decision logic in one tool {4;11}. Hence, neither is validated on its own, and the benefit of a decision mechanism cannot be separated from the quality of the forecast behind it."""),
("Surface fire spread is described by Rothermel", """Surface fire spread is described by Rothermel-type rate-of-spread models driven by fuel, moisture, wind, and slope {7;28}. These models are implemented in operational tools such as FlamMap {8} and in cellular simulators such as Cell2Fire {29}. The simulation core of VODA uses a model of the same family. Decision support built on these models ranges from strategic risk assessment {4;9} and multi-criteria allocation {10} to the integrated WFDSS platform {11}, whose field use has been studied extensively {13;14}. In all of them the decision logic is authored offline and the intervention menu is fixed. Fire management economics judges a response by the sum of two terms, the cost of the resources committed and the loss that still occurs {3}. The decision cost of VODA has the same two parts."""),
("Under time pressure, the weak point of decision support", """Under time pressure, the weak point of decision support is that decision analysis evaluates a given set of alternatives {36}. Option generation precedes choice yet is a neglected phase {18}, even though it can be formalized as a recursion of decision problems {37}, and systems that hard-wire the open parts of a problem are not adopted {20}. Cognitive evidence agrees, since generating options in ill-structured problems requires ideation beyond memory retrieval and raises the quality of the best option {38}, and operators build courses of action from recognized patterns rather than from menus {23}. Hence, a DSS should generate alternatives during operation and filter them by a strict, external evaluation."""),
("Mechanisms that generate alternatives share one pattern", """Mechanisms that generate alternatives share one pattern. Some search a fixed model for structurally different solutions {21}. Some combine modules under compatibility constraints {39}. Some add new actions to the action model at run time {40;41}. In every case the new alternatives are built from parts the system already understands. An open decision space is an open composition over a closed and verified basis. Mechanisms that change a rule base at run time follow the same pattern. Evolving TS models tune and add rules within the vocabulary {33;34}. Value-based selectors learn when a structural change is needed {42}. Generative models draft rules outside the current structure {43}. VODA uses all three, with tuning and selection in the closed layer and generation in the open layer, behind one satisficing acceptance rule {44} that keeps generation cheap to reject and the evaluator independent of the generator."""),
("Recent wildfire and disaster systems add learned", """Recent wildfire and disaster systems add learned and generative components but keep the intervention space closed or unchecked. Dubey and Dubey {45} map a Transformer forecast to four fixed response levels through expert rules. Chandra et al. {46} draw actions from a fixed ontology and check only their consistency. Cheng et al. {47} let LLMs weight a fixed set of indicators. Gerolimos et al. {48} call for open-world learning, but only for recognizing unseen events, not for responding to them. Multi-agent LLMs write free-text plans whose hallucinations are contained by retrieval, schemas, or post-hoc grading {49}. Cui et al. {51} argue for verification by physics-based simulation, yet confine their agent to a fixed tool library. WildfireKR {52} automates calibrated spread simulation but leaves intervention design to the analyst. None builds new interventions from effects a physical model can execute and lets that model decide admission. VODA fills this gap."""),
]
